## Readability — non-negotiable

**Source of truth:** `/Users/the1finix/Documents/GitHub/brain-box/school/methodology/readability-and-clarity.md`. Read it before drafting any chapter.

**Why this rule exists in this repo.** The Project Proposal (`First Report (Project Proposal).docx`) scored ~69%. The same writing-band penalties cost us 5/15 marks on the EEEM072 RSP proposal — group-writing cohort spread was **86 (Joel) / 80 (Talha) / 69 (us)**. The Interim Review Section "Step 8 — SPY 14 January 2022 BLOCKED trade" is the strongest writing we have produced — replicate that voice across the full dissertation.

> Earlier framing ("86 vs 73") conflated Joel's group mark with Fiyin's individual mark. Corrected: 86 vs 80 vs 69 on group writing; 97 vs 86 vs 73 on individual marks once peer review is factored in. The dissertation is **individual** (no peer-review lever) so only the writing rules below apply here.

**The "smart non-technical friend" test is mandatory.** A smart non-AI cousin must follow the abstract and Section 1. If they cannot, the chapter is not ready for the supervisor.

### Six rules (apply on every chapter)

1. **Lead with the intuition, follow with the formalism.** State the human consequence (preserving 95% of peak portfolio value during a crash) before any acronym. Three-acronym budget per paragraph; first use defines, subsequent uses revert to a plain noun ("the policy", "the forecaster", "the agent").
2. **One idea per sentence, ≤30 words for ≥4 of every 5 sentences.** Split anything with three commas or three "and"s.
3. **Define every acronym on first use; alternate with plain nouns afterwards.** No abstract should chain DRL / PPO / DeepAR / FinRL / GluonTS without breath.
4. **Signpost every section.** Open with one sentence stating scope. Close with one sentence linking to the next.
5. **Number-first results.** Open every results paragraph with the headline number + units, then mechanism, then decision (carried forward / discarded / further tested).
6. **Concrete worked example before abstract claim.** Every method section earns a walk-through of one day, one ticker, one decision — see Interim Review Step 8 for the model. Date, exact prices, exact threshold, exact decision.

### Mandatory pre-supervisor self-check

Run [`readability-and-clarity`](file:///Users/the1finix/Documents/GitHub/brain-box/school/methodology/readability-and-clarity.md) Section "Self-check" — six questions. If more than one fails, revise before sending.

### Editing-pass discipline

Allocate one full pass per chapter to the writing-band axes only: clear English, neat presentation (figure/table layout), correct referencing. EEEM072 evidence: this is where 5/15 marks bleed on first drafts.

## Document format: LaTeX-canonical · DOCX-editable

**Source of truth:** `/Users/the1finix/Documents/GitHub/brain-box/school/methodology/latex-docx-workflow.md`. Read it the first time you touch the dissertation.

This dissertation is written in LaTeX. The canonical source lives at:

```
report/main.tex          # canonical — agent edits this
report/main.docx         # editable mirror — user edits this
report/main.pdf          # compiled deliverable for the supervisor
report/refs.bib          # bibliography
report/figures/          # vector PDFs
```

Build commands:

```bash
cd report
latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex
pandoc main.tex --citeproc --bibliography=refs.bib -o main.docx
```

The edit loop:

1. Agent writes / updates `main.tex`. Regenerates `main.pdf` and `main.docx`.
2. User opens `main.docx`, marks it up (track-changes / inline comments).
3. User hands the marked-up DOCX back: *"incorporate these changes"*.
4. Agent runs `pandoc marked.docx -t latex -o marked.tex`, lists every change, applies each to `main.tex`, regenerates DOCX + PDF, and confirms in chat which changes were applied.

**Rules.**

- The agent **never edits the DOCX directly.** It only regenerates it from the current LaTeX.
- LaTeX wins on conflict, always.
- All equations are LaTeX (inline `$x$`, display `\[ \dots \]` or `align`). Number every equation referenced later.
- All cross-references use `\Cref{sec:foo}` from the `cleveref` package — never hand-typed numbers.

## Writing conventions

- **Never use the `§` glyph.** Write "Section 3" or "Sec. 3" in prose. In LaTeX use `\Cref{sec:foo}`. The user has explicitly flagged `§` as visually noisy.
- **One em-dash per sentence maximum.**
- **Smart quotes off in code blocks.** Smart quotes on in prose.
- **Numbered sections** use `1.`, `1.1`, `1.1.1` (not Roman numerals — Surrey dissertation convention).

